State of Massachusetts, } ss. Plymouth County, }
On this eighth day of November, in the year of our Lord one thousand eight hundred & forty three, personally appeared before Austin Packard, one of the Justices of the Peace in & for the County of Plymouth, & State of Massachusetts, Amy Tribou, a resident of North Bridgewater, in the said County of Plymouth, aged eighty three years, who, being first duly sworn, according to law, doth, on her oath, make the following declaration, in order to obtain the benefit of the provision made by the Act of Congress, passed March 3, 1843, entitled, "An Act granting a pension to certain revolutionary soldiers."
That she is the widow of William Tribou who was a private soldier in the Army of the Revolution; that she was a Pensioner under the Act of the 7 th of July 1838, & has received a pension under the last mentioned Act, at the rate of Sixty Six Dollars & Sixty Six Cents per annum; that she formerly had a Certificate entitling her to receive the same, signed by J. R. Poinsett, Secretary of War, dated April 25 th 1839, &

) Declaration. State of North Carolina } ss. County of Rockingham }
Before me a Justice of the peace in and for the aforesaid County and State, personally appeared Elizabeth Tucker , a resident of Said County and State, now aged about seventy eight (78) years, and to me well known as a highly respectable person, who, being first duly sworn according to law makes the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the law of Congress passed 4 th July 1836, 7 th July 1838, or any other law of Congress to which this her declaration may be applicable,
viz: That she is the widow of Shadrach Tucker deceased, a late private soldier and Revolutionary Pensioner; that her said husband was placed on the Pension Roll of the Agency of North Carolina about the first part of the year eighteen hundred and thirty three (1833) under the law of 7 th June 1832 at $30.00, thirty dollars per annum, for services as a private soldier in the North Carolina Militia in the war of the Revolution, and, after having been pensioned for a few months, died in the said County of Rockingham, on or about the twenty ninth (29 th ) day of December in the year eighteen hundred and thirty three (1833) leaving no children surviving him: That she was married to the said Shadrach Tucker in the said County, at her own father's house, on the fifteenth (15 th ) day of June in the year seventeen hundred and ninety three (1793) by one Thomas Moore a Baptist Minister who is now dead, and that her name previous to marriage was Elizabeth Tucker ; that she had, for many years before and after her said husband's death, a Family Bible, containing a Record of the said marriage and of the death of her said husband, and that said Family Bible having been不慎ly destroyed by the burning up of her house in said County about the 20 th day of October in the year eighteen hundred and thirty nine (1839), she has no record of her said marriage and no other proof than the affidavits of two old and respectable

State of New York } Jefferson County }
On this fifth day of April 1843 before the subscriber a Justice of the Peace in and for the county aforesaid personally appeared Molly Lapham — a resident of Watertown in the county of Jefferson and State of New York aged seventy nine — years, who being first duly sworn according to law, doth on her oath, makes the following declaration, in order to obtain the benefits of the provision made by the act of Congress, passed on the 3d March 1843, granting pensions to widows of persons who served during the Revolutionary War —
That she is the widow of Isaac Lapham and former widow of Charles Gulls who was a Private in Col. Baileys Regt. Massachusetts line and for whose services this declarant was pensioned under the act of July 7th 1838 and 23d August 1842 at the rate of forty six \frac{66}{100} dollars per annum — She further declares that she is still a widow
Sworn to and subscribed on the day and year above written. before me }
Phoebe Sabin Justice
Molly X Lapham mark
State of New York } Jefferson County }
I certify that I am personally acquainted with Molly Lapham the above named declarant, and that she is the widow of Isaac Lapham
Phoebe Sabin Justice
POST-MORTEM of the life and services of Molly Lapham

State of New York }
Dutchess County }
John Holmes of the town of Poughkeepsie in Dutchess County and State of New York aged 72 years personally appeared before me a Justice of the peace duly authorized to administer oaths for general purposes, who first being duly sworn according to law, Deposeth and saith, that is well acquainted with Amy Turner widow of Stephen Turner De d and have been well acquainted with both of the above named Stephen Turner and Amy Turner his wife for the last twenty five years or more. The said Stephen Turner was a pensioner of the United States at the rate of 20 per annum, and this deponent has been has been called upon to make out the power of attorney for the said Stephen Turner for at least ten years before his death, which took place on the 4 day of March 1848, and the reason why I know when he died is that deponent went prepared to make out his power of attorney on said 4 March and when I came to his house found him Dead therefore no business done. soon after say in 1849, the said Amy Turner widow of Stephen Turner De d applied for a Widows Pension, diligence was used to find the records of Rev d David Close, the clergeman that was said consummated said marriage and found none also for the town and church records but found none, upon which the family record was introduced of which this deponent retained a copy. V is
Account of S c . Stephen Turner and family first of himself was born in the year of the world Septem r 10 th 1761, and married to Amy Bennett July 31. 1781 - Caleb Turner born May 20 th 1782. Lydia Turner born Nov r 26 th 1784. John Turner born Feb y 19 th 1786. Amy Turner born Apr l 10 th 1788. Sarah Turner born May 10 1790. Stephen Turner born June 14 1793. Rhoda Turner born March 7 1798.

State of New York } County of Rensselaer }
Mary Van Benthysen a resident of Greenbush in the county & state aforesaid Apr. seventy nine has now made, who being first duly sworn according to law. Depose that she is the widow of Wm Van Benthysen late a pensioner of the United States, &c. And that she is now in the receipt of a pension at the rate of Eighty dollars per annum under the 2 d Section of the Act of Feb 3 d 1853. That having been informed & believes she is entitled to an increase of her pension on account of services rendered by her said husband in the Revolutionary War, part of the time as an Artillerist She therefore makes this claim upon the supposition that in the adjustment of her claim for a pension this class of service was not considered or included. In support of which claim she refers the pension Department to the evidence on file in that office or to such other evidence as may be furnished by her Agent Mr Valentine of Albany N.Y. who is hereby authorized to prosecute said claim & substitute such person at Washington as he may designate for the purpose. She further declares that she still remains a widow — — —
Subscribed & sworn to before me } this 22 nd day of February 1861 } Henry Goodrich Justice of the Peace }
her Mary X Van Benthysen mark
I certify that Mary Van Benthysen is the identical person above named and described, and being entirely blind is the reason she cannot write her name — — —
Henry Goodrich Justice of the Peace

State of New York } County of Rensselaer }
On this 13 th day of October 1864 before me Henry Goodrich a Justice of Peace in and for said county, personally appeared Mary Van Benthuyzen a pensioner of the United States, who being first duly sworn according to law, doth on her oath make the following Declaration in order to obtain the increase of pension which she is informed & believes was due her husband William Van Benthuyzen under the Act of Congress passed June 7 th 1832. That she is the widow of William Van Benthuyzen, who was an Artificer in the War of the Revolution, and a pensioner of the United States at the rate of Eighty dollars per annum under the Act aforesaid until the time of his death which was on or about the twenty third day of January, one thousand eight hundred and forty five. That this Declarant is now in the receipt of a pension at the rate of two hundred & forty dollars per annum under the 2 d section of the Act of Congress passed February 3 d 1853 In support of which claim she refers the Pension Department to the proof on file in that office. She further declares that she is now a widow Subscribed & sworn to before me } Mary X Van Benthuyzen this 13 th day of October 1864 } mark Henry Goodrich Justice of the Peace
I certify that Mary Van Benthuyzen is the identical person above named and described, and the reason of her not writing her name is because she is entirely blind, having lost her eye sight several years since, and has no interest whatever in the aforesaid claim Henry Goodrich Justice of the Peace

State of New York Chemung County ss } On the 27 th day of January 1844, before the subscriber a Judge of the County Courts of said County (the same being a Court of Records having a Seal and Clerk) personally appeared Agnes Van Gorden late a resident of Athens, Bradford County Penn a . now of the said County of Chemung aged Seventy five years and upwards who being first duly sworn according to law doth on her oath make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the provision made by the act of Congress passed 7 July 1838 entitled "An act granting half pay & pensions to certain widows", and an act approved the twenty third day of August 1842 amendatory thereof, and a resolution approved the sixteenth day of August 1842 entitled "A resolution declarative of the Pension Act of July seventh eighteen hundred & thirty eight";
That she is the widow of John Van Gorden who was a pensioner of the United States under the act of July 7 1832 for services as a private in the war of the Revolution (particularly stated in his declaration & the accompanying papers on file in the War Department) at the rate of Thirty three Dollars, thirty three Cents per annum:
She further declares that she was married to the said John Van Gorden at Shawangunk in the County of Ulster or Orange in the State of New York on the fifteenth day of October seventeen hundred ninety three by the Rev. Mr. Frecligh a Minister of the Presbyterian Church resident in that neighbor- hood: that her maiden name was Agnes Van Fleet. That her husband the aforesaid John Van Gorden died on the eighteenth day of June eighteen hundred thirty five: that she was not married to him prior to his leaving the services, but the marriage took place previous to the first day of January seventeen hundred and ninety four: viz at the time above stated: That she still remains the widow of the said John Van Gorden, having never married since his death: That her application has been delayed in consequence of unsuccessful searches for the record of the clergyman who performed the marriage ceremony. tho' she employed counsel to present her application

State of New York { Dutchess County ss. }
On this ninth day of June one thousand eight hundred and thirty eight personally appeared before me Daniel D. Stein a Judge of the court of Common Pleas of said County of Dutchess Margaret Van Vleck a resident of the Town of La Grange in said County aged eighty one years, who being first duly sworn according to Law doth on her oath make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the provision made by the act of Congress passed July fourth one thousand eight hundred and thirty six - That she is the widow of Abraham Van Vleck, who was a private soldier in the militia of the State of New York and served in the revolutionary army and was a pensioner under the act of June second one thousand eight hundred and thirty two and that he had in his possession at the time of his death a Certificate of pension given at the war office of the United States the twenty fifth day of October one thousand eight hundred and thirty three of which the following is a true Copy -
War department revolutionary claim - I certify that in conformity with the law of the United States of the 7th. June 1832 Abraham Van Vleck of the State of New York, who was a private in the revolutionary army is entitled to receive fifty five dollars and seventy five cents per annum during his natural life, commencing on the 4th. of March 1831 and payable semiannually on the 4th. of March and 4th. of September in every year Given at the war office of the United States this 25 day of October one thousand eight hundred and thirty three. Genl. Cass Secretary of war Examined and Countersigned J. S. Edwards Commissioner of Pensions. She further declares that she was married to the said Abraham Van Vleck

5764  New York  Joan Vervalen widow of Cornelius Vervalen N.J.  who was a pensioner under the Act of and who died on the 19 th April 1820 of New York City in the State of New York who was a Private in the Comp y commanded by Captain Blanch of the Reg t commanded by Col Reg in the New Jersey line for 15 months days
Inscribed on the Roll of New York at the rate of 51 Dollars ————— Cents per annum, to commence on the 4th day of March, 1836.
