State Of Tennessee } County Of Davidson }
On this 5 th day of March A. D. eighteen hundred and fifty three personally appeared before me an acting justice of the peace, in and for the County and State aforesaid Mrs Linah Vick a resident of Davidson County in the State of Tennessee, aged Eighty four years who being duly sworn according to law, doth, in her oath make the following declaration, in order to obtain the benefit of the provisions made by the act of Congress passed February 3 d 1853 Section the second That she is the widow of Joseph Vick a deceased Revolutionary pensioner, whose name is on the pension roll at Nashville Tennessee, and that her husband, the said Vick drew at the rate of forty six dollars 88 cts per annum, and that she drew the arrearages of her husband's pension up to the day of his death, at the Pension Office at Nashville Tennessee — She further declares that she was married to the said Joseph Vick to the best of her recollection on the fifteenth day of November the year eighteen hundred and and twenty two. That her husband, the aforesaid Joseph Vick died on the sixteenth day of December 1844 that she was not married to him prior to his leaving the service, but that the marriage took place subsequent to the second day of January eighteen hundred, viz at the time above stated. She further declares that she is now a widow, and that she has never before made any application for a pension —
Sworn to and subscribed

State of New York } ss Otsego County }
On This nineteenth day of May In the year of Our Lord one Thousand Eight Hundred and Forty Three Personally Appeared before the Subscriber A Justice of the Peace in and for Said County of Otsego Sarah Walling A Resident of the Town of Oneonta in the County of Otsego Aged Seventy one years Who being first Dulily Sworn According to Law doth on her Oath make the following declaration in Order to obtain the benefits of the provision Made by the act of Congress passed on the 3 rd day of March 1843. Granting Pensions to Widows of Persons who served during the Revolutionary War That She is the Widow of Simeon - Walling who was A private in the Army of the Revolution but in what Regiment he served in or what offices his said Husband served under this defomat is not Able to say And the Annual Amount of the Pension Which She Received under the act of July 7 th 1838 was Thirty Dollars Per annum - She Further declares that She is still a Widow Sworn to and Subscribed on the day and year Above Written before her Sarah Walling James McCall Justice of the peace mark
I hereby Certify That I am Personally Acquainted with the Above Sarah Walling who made the Above declarati- on in order to obtain a pension for one year and have been for a number of years and that She sustains a good moral character in the Neighborhood in which She Lives James McCall Justice of the peace

UNITED STATES OF AMERICA.
State of Ohio } ss. County of Guernsey }
Be it known that before me, the undersigned, a Justice of the Peace, duly authorized by law to administer oaths within and for the County aforesaid, personally appeared Catherine Walters and made oath in due form of law that she is the Widow of the identical John Walters who drew a pension under Acts of Congress of 1818 and May 1828 at the rate of $ 96 & 2/3 per annum, on account of the Revolutionary services of Said John Walters
That she makes this affidavit for the purpose of obtaining increase of said pension under Act of July 15 th 1828 and same under Act of March 3 rd 1855
Sworn to and subscribed on the 13 th day of April 1855, before me. }
Catharine Walters her mark
Robt Marshall , Justice of the Peace.
State of Ohio } ss. County of Guernsey }
On this 13 th day of April , 1855, personally appeared before me, the undersigned, a Justice of the Peace, duly authorized by law to administer oaths in and for the County aforesaid, George W Walters and Rachel Walters and made oath in due form of law that they were personally acquainted with John Walters , deceased, Revolutionary pensioner, formerly of Allegany Co., Penn a and late of Guernsey Co., Ohio that he drew a pension at the rate of $ 96 & 2/3 dollars per annum. That he was at the time of his death a resident of Guernsey County Ohio , that he died on the 30 th day of March , 1829, and that there now survives the following named his  Widow Catharine Walters and that they are not interested
Sworn to and subscribed before me the day and year above written. }
George Walters Rachel Walters
Robt Marshall , Justice of the Peace.
2 Witnesses here

State of Virginia } SS County of Accomack }
On this seventh day of April A.D. 1855, personally appeared before me, a Justice of the peace within and for the County and State aforesaid Sabra P. Naples, aged sixty four years, a resident of Accomack County, in the State of Virginia, who being duly sworn according to law, declares that she is the widow of Samuel Naples, deceased, who was a lieutenant of Infantry in the company commanded by Captain Thomas Mead in the ninth Virginia regiment of the Continental line commanded by Colonel George Mathews, in the revolutionary war; that her said husband obtained a pension from the United States for his services in said war under the act of Congress passed the day of A.D. 1828; that since his death she has filed her declaration as the widow of said Samuel Naples deceased and has been placed upon the pension list at the rate of $320. per annum under the act of Congress of the third day of February A.D. 1853; that she refers for evidence of the services of her said husband, and for the proof of his death, of her marriage, and of her being his widow, to the declaration of her said husband made to obtain a pension aforesaid, to the declaration made by herself as aforesaid, and to the papers accompanying said declarations now on file in the pension office.
She further states that she was married to the said Samuel Naples in the County and State aforesaid on the 20 th day of August A.D. 1822 by one Thomas Walters a minister of the Gospel and that her name before her said marriage was Sabra P. Searburgh that her

660 Massachusetts Margaret Wardwell, dec d widow of Benjamin Wardwell, who died on the 1 st in the State of who was a Artificer in the                      commanded by Captain                      of the                      commanded by                      in the Penn a line for 2 years due pro $144.00 for pay Inscribed on the Roll of Boston Mass at the rate of 240 Dollars                      Cents per annum, to commence on the 4 th day of March, 1831, and ending on the 15 th April 1837.
