Declaration
State of North Carolina } County of Rutherford } On this 16 th day of November, 1848. personally appeared before the Court of Pleas and Quarter Sessions now opened and held at the Court House in Rutherfordton, for the County & State aforesaid, James Watson, a resident of the County of Rutherford, and a Son of Jane Watson late dec d who being first, duly sworn according to law, doth, on his oath make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefits of the provision made by the act of Congress passed on the 2 d February, 1848 (1848) entitled an act making further provision for the widows of persons who served during the Revolutionary war.
That this declarant the aforesaid James Watson in the behalf of himself & the other heirs of the said Jane Watson dec d States, that the said Jane Watson was the widow of John Watson who was a Private & Captain in the Revolutionary war and such he served the United States against the common enemy. That the said Jane Watson dec d was a Pensioner under the act of the 17 th June, 1844, at the rate of $76.66 per annum, and died on the 29 th day of October, 1848, not having remarried since the death of her said husband John Watson dec d and that this declaration is made for the purpose of obtaining the arrears of pension due under the act above named, that the act of 2 d Feby: 1848. Sworn to and subscribed on the day & year first above written before M c . Logan, J. } James Watson the Court }

United States of America
State of Georgia } ss- Walton County }
Be it known that on this 26 th day of March 1845 before me James Lindley a Justice of the Peace in and for the County and State aforesaid personally appeared Mrs Ailsy Webb and made oath in due form of Law That she is the widow of Austin Webb who was a pensioner of the United States under an act of Congress passed on the 7 th day of June 1832 on the Roll of the Georgia Aged at the rate of $80. 00 per annum in Walton County State of Georgia and died on the 1 st day of July 1838. And that she is the identical person who made application for a pension for the Revolutionary services of her deceased husband under the act of Congress of the 7 July 1838 entitled An act granting half pay and pensions to certain widows. That she obtained a small portion of the benefit of the provisions made by said act to wit; from the day of her said husbands death That she has been informed that she was denied the full benefits of said act by the construction given the act of Congress of the 30 th April 1844 entitled An act making appropriations for the payment of Revolutionary and other Pensioners of the United States for the fiscal year ending 30 June 1845 which act she is informed declares that no widow shall hereafter receive a pension for the same time that her husband drew a pension That she is now informed that by a late resolution passed by Congress its Resolved, That the aforesaid act of 30 April 1844 shall not be so constructed as to effect the Claims of those widows whose application for a pension shall have been made and filed in the Pension office awaiting the decision of the Commissioner on Pensions That her Declaration proof of Identity and marriage was made under the act of 7 July 1838 and filed with the Commissioner on Pensions awaiting his decision thereon prior to the 30 th of April 1844 That she claims the benefit of the provision made by the act of Congress passed on the 20 August 1842 declaratory of the act of the 7 July 1838. and prays that no widow shall be barred from the benefits of the act of 7 July 1838 if she be a widow at the time of her application and is entitled in other respects She holds that no export facts Law should be put by Congress barring her claims And makes the above statements of the laws in force from information in order to obtain the pension she considers due her under the act of 7 July 1838 to the day of her husbands death viz from the 1 st of

The State of Ohio } In open Court Franklin County ss }
On this 6th day of January A.D. 1887, before the Court of Probate held within and for the County of Franklin aforesaid personally appeared Rhoda Webb aged 60 years, a resident of Gastons in the County and State aforesaid, who being duly sworn according to law, doth, on her oath, make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the provision made by act of Congress, passed on the 3d, February, 1853, granting pensions to widows of persons who served during the Revolutionary War, that she is the widow of Josiah Webb, deceased, who was a soldier in the Revolutionary War, and the identical Josiah Webb who was placed on the Pension Roll of the State of Ohio for such service in the Revolutionary War, at the rate of $26.00 per annum payable at the Pittsburgh Agency in the State of Pennsylvania.
She further declares that she was married to said Josiah Webb in Warren in the State of Connecticut on the first day of September, 1822, by Judah Eldred a Justice of the peace, and that her name before said marriage was Rhoda Page; that her said husband died at Gastons Franklin County aforesaid, on the 23d day of January 1842; That she was not married to him prior to the

State of Kentucky Boyle County } ss
On this the 13 th day of June 1843 Personally appeared before me Abram F.ckerson an acting Justice of the Peace in and for the County of Boyle State of Kentucky Mrs Lucy R Webb aged about twenty five years and a resident of the said County and who after being first duly sworn according to Law doth on her Oath make the following Declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the provisions made by the act of Congress passed the 3 d day of March 1843 Granting Pensions to the widows of certain Persons who served in the Revolutionary war Namely That she is the widow of Lewis Webb deceased who was a Captain in the war of the Revolution and belonged to the Virginia Line and served many years and on account which served he received Pension of under the act of 7 June 1832 of $480. per annum. She further declares that she on the 16 th day of March 1843 made her declaration before the aforesaid Justice, in order to obtain a Pension under the act of 7 July 1838 and now refers to the evidence composing the matter of the application of her said husband for said Pension and the evidence composing the matter of her application for said Pension for such facts as are necessary in the investigation of her claim. She further declares that she has not been married since the death of her said husband but still continues his widow.
sworn to and subscribed before me A F.ckerson Justice } Lucy R Webb }

State of New York } Oneida County } }
On this ninth day of November, one thousand Eight Hundred and Fifty, before me, Abel E Chandler, a Justice of the Peace, in and for Said County, personally appeared Susanna Weeks and made oath in due form of law, that she is the Widow of Uriah Weeks, who was a Revolutionary pensioner; that she is now on the roll of the Albany Agency at the rate of Eighty Dollars per Annum; that she believes herself entitled to an increase of pension, and asks whatever may be found due her under any of the acts, and resolutions, which are applicable to her case,
Subscribed and sworn } before me this ninth } day of November 1850. }
her Susanna X Weeks mark
A. E. Chandler Justice of the peace, of Oneida Co., N.Y.

Declaration - In order to obtain the benefits of the 7 th Section of the act of Feb. 3, 1853
State of Maine } County of Penobscot }
On this seventeenth day of September A.D. 1853 personally appeared before the Sup. Judicial Court of said County & State Patience York a Resident of Maine in the County of Penobscot & State aforesaid aged sixty six years who being first duly sworn according to law doth on her oath, make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefits of the provision made by the act of Congress passed on the 3 rd of February 1853 gran- ting pensions to widows of persons who serv- ed during the Revolution; and that she is the Widow of Solomon York who was a private in the army of the Revolution and served another year but the patriot, not known and was a pensioner at the rate of forty eight dollars per annum on the Roll of the Maine Agency. She further declares that she was married to the said Solomon York on the fourteenth day of July Eighteen hundred twenty nine that her said Husband died on the tenth

State of North Carolina } S.S. County of Stokes }
On this 24 th day of September 1849 personally appeared before me J.M. Covington a Justice of the peace in and for the County of Stokes aforesaid - Nancy Ziglar a resident of said County aged 83 or 84 years who being first duly sworn according to law doth on her oath make the following Declaration, in order to obtain the provisions & benefits made by the acts of Congress passed on the 7 th July 1838 and 7 th February 1848 granting pension to widows of persons who served during the Revolutionary war. That she is the widow of Leonard Ziglar deceased, and from bodily infirmity is rendered unable to travel to the Court of said County to make this her declaration without endangering her health, She states that her husband was a private soldier in the war of the Revolution, and drew a pension at the rate of thirty five dollars & forty four cents per annum from the 4 th of March 1831 and that he died in the County of Stokes aforesaid on 10 th of August 1849 his Pension Certificate was dated the 7 th of May 1833 & Numbered 12540. signed by John Robb acting Secretary of War which Certificate this applicant has recently returned to the agent of the U. States for paying pensions in Fayetteville N. Carolina. She further declares that she was married to the said Leonard Ziglar in the County of Culpeper & Commonwealth of Virginia in the year 1783 or 1784 by a Rev. She does not know his name which will appear by the proof herunto annexed, and for the purpose of establishing her claim for a pension begs leave to refer the Pension department to the declaration and proof of her husband filed therein by which he obtained his pension. She is a widow and only became so the 10 th of last month -
Sworn to and subscribed the date first above written before me J.M. Covington Nancy Ziglar her mark

No 6790
Sally West, Widow of Joseph West Essex Co. New York
Admitted, Act Feb 3. 1853 at rate of $23.11 c per annum, commencing on date of said act
