State of Pennsylvania } Cumberland County }
On this twelfth day of Decem- ber, in the year one Thousand eight hundred and forty-nine, personally appeared before the un- designed a Justice of the Peace, in and for the County and State aforesaid, duly com- missioned and sworn, Mrs Elizabeth Snyder a resident of Allen Township, County and State aforesaid, aged sixty-six years on the third day of August last past, who being first duly sworn, doth depose and say, that she is of the age above stated, viz sixty- six years on the third day of August last, having been born as she has always understood and doth verily believe to be the fact, on the third day of August in the year seventeen hundred and eighty- three; that she is the oldest child of Conrad and Feronica (sometimes written and called Fanny or Francis) Weaver, always having and understood and believed herself to be their child; that her father the afore- said Conrad Weaver is now deceased, having died in the County of Lancaster, in the State of Penn- sylvania in which County he had resided for many years, prior to his death, and at the date of his decease drew a pension from the United States at the rate of One hundred dollars per annum, for his services in the War of the Revolution — That her Mother, Feronica Weaver, above named, and widow as above stated of Conrad Weaver, deceased, has for a number of years resided with this deponent in Allen Township, Cumberland County, State afore- said — That she, this deponent, was married in the year one Thousand eight hundred and five, when she was in the twenty-second year of her age, and has a daughter now living and married having children, who is now about forty-five years of age; that her mother, Feronica Weaver above stated, still remains the widow of her de- ceased husband, Conrad Weaver above stated, and still resides with this deponent.
In presence of Geo H. Coeclin - Catharine Ringman }
her Elizabeth + Snyder mark
Sworn to and subscribed before me The day and year first above mentioned, (viz (12 th December 1849) and I hereby certify that the deponent is a highly

State of New York } ss. Cayuga County, }
On this sixteenth day of March, in the year eighteen hundred and fifty-four, personally appeared before me, George Thompson, County Judge of Cayuga County Courts, Dorcas Wheden, a resident of the city of Auburn, in the county and State aforesaid, aged sixty-eight years, who, first being sworn according to law, doth, on her oath, make the following declaration, in order to obtain the benefits of the provision made by the act of Congress, passed on the third of February, eighteen hundred and fifty-three, granting pensions to widows of persons who served during the Revolutionary war:
That she is the widow of Edmund Wheden, who was a Revolutionary pensioner of the United States for several years prior to his death; that he received his pension payments from the Pension Agency at Albany, in the State of New York; that since the death of the said Edmund Wheden, she has applied for the arrears of his said pension, due at the time of his death, and received the same from the Pension Agency at Albany, aforesaid; that she is unable to state under what act of Congress the pension of the said Edmund Wheden was allowed, nor the amount he drew per annum, because she believes the certificate thereof was sent to the Pension Agency at Albany at the time she received the arrears of the said Edmund

State of New Hampshire, County of Grafton ss. }
On this sixteenth day of April Anno Domini 1853, personally appeared before me, Charles R. Morrison, a Circuit Justice of the Court of Common Pleas for said State of New Hampshire, Widow Eunice Wheeler a resident of Perimont in the County of Grafton aforesaid, aged twenty eight years, who being duly sworn according to law, doth, on her oath make the following declaration, in order to obtain the benefit of the provisions, made by the act of Congress passed February third Anno Domini 1853, - that she is the widow of Periah Wheeler late of Jamaica in Windham County, Vermont, who was a Revolutionary Pensioner at the Vermont agency at the rate of one hundred two dollars and eighty three cents per annum. -
She further declares, that she was married to the said Periah Wheeler on the nineteenth day of January Anno Domini 1815, and that her said husband, the aforesaid Periah Wheeler died on the sixteenth day of December Anno Domini 1836, as will appear by evidence on file in Pension office at Washington that she was married to him at the time above stated. She further swears, that she was a widow at the passage of the act, and is still a widow that about three years ago she supposes  sent Chester Ben son for her forwarded some papers to Washington in regard to a pension, but their nature is cannot distinctly be remembered & I know not that they were sent. attest. Jonas D. Beebe Sworn to and subscribed on the day & year Eunice Wheeler first above written, and I hereby certify that I believe that the said Eunice Wheeler, on account of bodily infirmity is unable to appear in open Court, C. R. Morrison { Circuit Justice of the Court of Common Pleas for the State of New Hampshire

State of Vermont } }
Addison County }
On this 4 th day of March AD 1887. Personally appeared before the subscriber a justice of the Peace for said County Anna White a resident of Ferrisburgh in said County aged eighty one years and after being duly sworn according to law doth on her oath make the following declaration in order to obtain an increase under the law of July 7. 1838. & subsequent acts in relation thereto. That she is the widow of David White late of Bennington County Vermont deceased who was a pensioner of the United States under the law of March 18. 1818. for service rendered in the Navy. That subsequently she has been placed on the pension roll at the rate of thirty three dollars & thirty three cents under the law of July 7. 1838. & subsequent acts and on the 15 th day of January 1857. was inscribed on the pension roll at the rate of thirty six dollars per annum & is now in the receipt of said pension.
She further states that her husband the said David of her told her that he entered the army of the Revolution from the town of Peterborough New Hampshire and served one campaign as a private in the army and then entered the Navy on board the ship Hancock in which service he continued for a period of two years or thereabouts, about six months of the time as a prisoner of war. That the privations of the prisoners were so great that many of the prisoners died. He often spoke of having been engaged in two naval battles in one of which the enemy were taken in the other he was taken prisoner and detained as above stated, She cannot fully identify

Declaration.
