State of North Carolina } Union County }
personally appeared before me James Marsh one of judges of the court of sessions and quarter sessions for said County Celia Williams a resident of the County & State aforesaid aged 85 years who being first duly sworn according to Law doth on her oath make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the provision made by the act of July 7 th 1838 and act 23 rd August 1842 embracing March the 3 rd 1843 & 17 th June 1844. Granting half pay and pensions to certain widows. that she is the widow of Job Williams decd. who was a pensioner of the United States at Forty dollars per annum She further declares that she was married to the said in Robb's County N.C. Job Williams in the 18 th year of her age & that she was born on the 15 th day of January 1762. as my parents told me. that Abraham Baker a clergyman married her & her husband. & that her husband the aforesaid Job Williams died on the 7 th day of October 1842. that she was mar- ried to him before January the 1 st 1794. to wit at the time a born slave. that she yet remains a widow Sworn & subscribed this 3 rd day of July 1847 Jas Marsh Celia Williams
I further certify hereby that Celia Williams mark who has sworn & subscribed to the above declaration is an old lady of fine character for veracity & that her statement is entitled to full credit and from her looks & appearance she is at least 85 years of age and is helpless & blind & cannot travel to the court house Given under my hand this 3 rd day of July 1847. Jas. Marsh

State of Virginia } Court; County of Chesterfield }
On this 23 rd day of April  May 1856 personally appeared before me Jas H Moody a justice of the peace, duly authorized to administer oaths within and for the County and State aforesaid. Healey Cole, a resident of Chesterfield County who being duly sworn according to law, doth on his oath make the following declaration in order to obtain the provisions made by the act of Congress passed 29 th July 1828; That he is the administrator on the estate of Mary Williams deceased, the widow of John Williams a Revolutionary Pensioner from Chesterfield County under act 7 th June 1832 at the rate of $66, 66 per annum, and who died on the 8 th September 1842; That this declaration is made for the purpose of receiving what money is due the estate of the said Mary Williams under the act 29 th July 1828;
That John Williams the aforesaid Pensioner married Mary Bottoms in Chesterfield County in the year 1797 or 1798 by Rev. Ebenezer Clegg a minister of the Gospel, and John Williams died as before stated 8 th September 1842; His widow Mary Williams died on the 10 th day of October 1853 without applying for or receiving any pension so far as he knows or believes under said act June 29 th July 1828;
That the following are the names of the children of the said John Williams (the Pensioner) and Mary Williams his wife, who, were living at the time the mother died in the year 1853: To wit: Samuel Williams Pleasant G Williams, Sarah Vest, wife of James Vest, and Martha S Snellings, wife of Peter Snellings, That Sarah Vest, and James Vest, her husband have both died, since the death of Mary Williams the mother of Sarah Vest leaving the following children: To wit: Richard F Vest, Thomas J Vest and John W Vest;
The following heirs are supposed to be then entitled under act 29 th July 1828; Being the children and grandchildren of the said John Williams the Pensioner and his wife Mary Williams both dead To wit: Samuel Williams, Pleasant G Williams, & Martha S Snellings, children Richard F Vest, Thomas J Vest John W Vest grandchildren -
For further information as to marriage of John Williams with his wife Mary Bottoms, the death of the parties in question, and to his being the lawful Administrator will more fully appear by reference to the proof hereto annexed
Healey Cole
Administrator

