On this eighth day of September, 1838, before the Hon. Benjamin Walworth, one of the Judges of the Court of Common Pleas in & for the County of Chautauqua in the State of New York, (the same being a Court of Record,) personally appeared Candace Sage , a resident of the town of Pompey in the County and State aforesaid, in the 80 th year of her age, who being first duly sworn according to law, doth on her oath make the following Declaration in order to obtain the benefits of the provision made by the Act of Congress, passed July 7. 1838, entitled "An Act granting half pay and pensions to certain widows": — That she is the Widow of Abraham Sage , who was a Private in the service of the United States in the War of the Revolution; that sufficient proof of the services of the said Abraham Sage will appear by reference to the Records of the War Department, & his Certificate (N o 8825,) granting him Sixty three dollars and thirty three cents per Annum, during his natural life; which Certificate is now on file, with other papers, in the Office of the Secretary of War.
She further declares that she was married to the said Abraham Sage , at the dwelling house of Ephraim Hollister, (her father,) in Berlin, Connecticut, on the 25 th day of June, 1778, and that her husband, the aforesaid Abraham Sage , died at Paris, N. Y. on the 27 th day of December, 1836, and that she has remained a Widow ever since that period; that, as she believes, she was not married to the said Abraham Sage prior to his leaving the service, for which he obtained his pension, but the marriage took place previous to the first of January, Seventeen hundred & ninety four,

