On this third day of November AD 1857, before the subscriber an associate Judge of the Court of Common Pleas of Bedford County aforesaid, in the state aforesaid, personally came Jane Longsmith, an inmate of the Almshouse & a resident of the County and State aforesaid, age about eighty eight years, who being first duly sworn according to law, doth on her oath make the following declaration, in order to obtain the benefits of the provision made by the Act of Congress, passed on the 3 d . February, 1853, granting pensions to widows of persons who served during the Revolutionary War; that she is the widow of Martin Longsmith, who was a soldier of the Revolution but that she cannot say any thing of his services, never having received any information about them. That for his services her said husband had a pension certificate issued under the act of 7 th June, 1832, to him, dated the 8 th day of July, A.D. 1834, signed by Levi Cass, Secretary of War, allowing him a pension of twenty six dollars & sixty six cents per annum, which pension he received up to the time at the Agency in Philadelphia, Pa of his death, & that she respectfully refers to the declaration made by her said husband, with the accompanying proofs, now on file in the pension office.
She further declares that she was married to the said Martin Longsmith in Bedford Township, Bedford County Pennsylvania, on the 9 th day of May, A.D. 1834, by one William Crisman Esq a Justice of the Peace; that her name before her said marriage was Jane Smith; that her said husband died on the 13 th day of October, one thousand eight hundred and forty one, and that she was not married to him prior to the deems of January, eighteen hundred, but at the time above stated.

no 6957.
Jane Longstreth.
