On this 20 th Day of December A.D. 1842 Personally appeared before me the Subscriber a Justice of the Peace in and for the County aforesaid. Mrs. Alotta Osborn a resident of Southbury in said County aged 90 Years, who being first duly sworn according to Law, doth on her Oath make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the provision made by the Acts of Congress passed July 7 th 1838 and the 23 rd of August 1842. granting Pensions to Widows of Persons who served during the Revolutionary War. That She is the Widow of Shadrach Osborn deceased, who was an Assistant Commissary of Issues and Purchases in the War of the Revolution, and a Pensioner of the United States under the Act of June 7 th 1832 on the Connecticut Agency at the rate of $320 per Annum, and after the death of the said Shadrach Osborn which took place on the 27 th Day of August 1838 as appears by evidence on file in the Pension Office. And upon application being made as his Widow to an increase of rate of Pension from the 24 th March 1831 to the 27 th of August 1838 it will appear by reference to the papers, the claim was allowed and paid at the rate of $600 per Annum in right of the Widow the present applicant - And She further declares, that after the death of her Husband, she made application to the War Department for the benefit of the Act of 4 th July 1836, the claim being disallowed, and the application still remaining on file begs respectfully to refer to the Papers for the evidence of Marriage which took place on the 15 th Day of October A.D. 1793 And the Papers of her late Husband under Act of 1832 for the proof of service which will show to have been upwards of four Years as an Assistant Commissary of Issues and Purchases - And hereby relinquishes all claim to the Act of 4 th July 1836 and resumes the application for a Pension under the Act of 7 th July 1838 and the Act of 23 rd August 1842 at the rate of $600 per Annum as the Widow of said Shadrach Osborn to whom she was married before the 1 st of January A.D. 1794 -
Alotta Osborn

