On this 2 d day of October, 1838, before the Hon. Benjamin Walworth, one of the Judges of the Court of Common Pleas in & for the County of Chautauque in the State of New York (the same being a Court of Record,) personally appeared Mehitabel Spencer , a resident of the town of Villinova in the County and State aforesaid, aged Seventy six years on the 15 th day of April last, who being first duly sworn according to law, doth on her Oath, make the following Declaration, in Order to obtain the benefit of the provision made by the Act of Congress, passed July 7. 1838, intitled, "An Act granting half pay and pensions to certain Widows." - That she is the Widow of Reuben Spencer , who was a Private in the Service of the United States in the War of the Revolution, and whose Pension Certificate, allowing him fifty four dollars per Annum, during life, is hereto annexed - And that for further proof of his services, she refers to the Records of the War Department - And she further declares that she was married to the said Reuben Spencer, at East Haddam, in the State of Connecticut on the 21 st day of November, 1792, by the Rev. Simson Dickinson, a Baptist Minister of that town - And for further proof as to her marriage, she refers to the annexed paper marked "A." which is in the hand writing of the said Reuben Spencer, her late husband - and which she declares was taken out of the family Bible - and for like proof, she also refers to the testimony hereto annexed -
And she further declares that her husband the aforesaid Reuben Spencer, died at Villinova aforesaid, on the 9 th day of April, 1836; that she has remained a Widow ever since his death, and is now a Widow - and that she was not married

