On this sixteenth day of January, A. D. 1834, personally appeared before me James Gregory — a Justice of the Peace within and for the said County of Essex, Devereux D. Bartlett, a resident of said Marblehead, aged forty-seven years, who being first duly sworn according to law, doth on his oath testify and declare, that he is a son and was formerly Guardian of Tabitha Bartlett , late of said Marblehead, widow, deceased, intestate: that said Tabitha Bartlett was at the time of her decease the widow of William Bartlett , who was in much service both by land and sea in the War of the Revolution: that among other service he was a Sergeant in what was called the eight months' service in the year 1775 — in the Company of Capt John Merrill of the 21 st Regiment of Massachusetts Foot, commanded by Colonel John Glover, — for which period of service his said late mother was pensioned on the 23 d of December, 1837, to the amount of thirty-seven dollars per annum — under the provisions of the law of July 24 th , 1836.
He further declares, that the Declaration upon which his said Mother was Pensioned was signed by himself as her Guardian aforesaid, — she having lost her faculties through old age and infirmities at the time it was so signed: that he was aware when he so signed said Declaration, both from the testimony of aged persons of his acquaintance and from what he himself could recollect of the statements of his said late father, William Bartlett aforesaid, that he was in much other service in the said War, beside that for which his mother was Pensioned as above set forth; but not knowing the particulars of it much of it was not set forth in said Declaration.

