Be it Known, That on this 4 th day of June 1851, personally appeared before the undersigned, a Justice of the Peace in and for said County and State, John Hyell, aged fifty three years, a resident of Lincoln County aforesaid, who, on his oath, and for the following Declaration in order to obtain from the United States the amount of Pension, which was due Rebecca Benson, deceased, Widow of Levin Benson dec d , at the time of her death, under Acts of Congress passed July 4 th 1838, March 3 d 1843, June 17 th 1844, and February 2 d 1848, granting Pension to certain Widows, That he is the Husband of Rebecca Benson, who is the tenth child of the said Levin and Rebecca Benson deceased, although two of the elder children were twins, That the said Levin Benson was a Pensioner of the United States on the Roll of the Nashville Tennessee Agency under Act of Congress of June 4 th 1832 at the rate of Eighty Dollars per annum for services rendered the United States in the Delaware Line, Army of the Revolution, that the said Levin Benson was for forty years before his death a resident of Lincoln County State of Tennessee, that he continued to receive his Pension, as aforesaid until his death, which took place in said Lincoln County on or about the 13 th day of June in the year Eighteen Hundred and thirty six (1836), that he left a Widow, the aforesaid Rebecca Benson, who survived him, and remained his Widow until the 16 th day of October Eighteen Hundred and fifty, (1850), when she died in Henderson County Pennsylv a , that previous to her death, she did not receive a Pension in right of her husband, the aforesaid Levin Benson, although at one time previous to her death she attempted to have an application therefor prepared , but Declarant does not believe that it was ever forwarded or presented to the Department at Washington City; Declarant has here stated the the facts in relation to said claim, as of his own knowledge, and will now proceed to give the following statement of facts in relation thereto, as he has been informed by, and has always understood, from the said Levin Benson and his wife the said Rebecca Benson deceased, to wit;
That the said Levin Benson was a Soldier in the Revolutionary War, and in the Delaware Line, that shortly after the close of the Revolutionary War, he with his father removed from Delaware, to the County of Rowan in the State of North Carolina, where they settled that sometime thereafter, as Declarant recollects, the said Levin Benson, being then a Widow, with three children, intermarried with Rebecca Brummel, which was in the year as affiant now recollects having heard them say, Seventeen Hundred and eighty four or Seventeen Hundred and eighty five (1784 or 1785) that they were married by a minister

