On this 3 rd day of May A.D. 1853 personally appeared before me Ephraim Packard, Judge of the Probate Court in & for the County of Piscataquis - Eleanor Burrill, a resident of Quispa in the State of Maine, aged 65 years who just being duly sworn, according to law, doth, on her oath make the following Declaration in order to obtain the benefits of the provision made by the Act of Congress, passed the 3 rd day of February A.D. 1853, granting pensions to widows of persons who served during the Revolutionary war; that she is the widow of John Burrill, late of Sangerville, deceased, who
in the company commanded by Capt. in the Revolutionary war; that at the time of her said husband's decease, he was a Revolutionary Pensioner of the United States, and in the receipt of the sum of Ninety six Dollars per annum, receiving his pay or pension aforesaid from the Agent for paying pensions at Portland in the State of Maine, and she refers to her said husband's application for a Pension, now on file in the pension office at Washington,

New York } County of } }
that Butler being duly sworn, says he resides in South Bridge the county and state aforesaid, is eighty years of age, and that he now receives a pension for his services, as a soldier, in the army of the Revolution, from the Government of the United States, of ninety six dollars per annum, under an act of Congress passed in 1818. that he is well acquainted with Cata Butler, that she is the widow of Stephen Butler, youngest Brother of this deponent. that he was present at the marriage of his brother Stephen Butler with the said Cata Butler - that her maiden name was Perkins. that the marriage took place in the town of Argyle in the county of Washington state aforesaid in the fall of the year 1792. that his brother Stephen Butler died in the month of October 1812. that the said Cata Butler is now, and has remained, the widow of his brother Stephen since his death.
This deponent further says, that his brother Stephen Butler was a soldier in the army of the revolution - that his brother Stephen Butler enlisted into the army of the revolution in the spring or early part of summer of 1777. in the town of Enfield in the county of Hartford state of Connecticut, in a company commanded by Captain Abbey, in Colonel Miller's regiment, in General Parsons' Brigade. that at the time he enlisted, resided in the town of Stafford, Windham County, the state of Connecticut. that his brother Stephen, immediately after his enlistment, was sent to Hartford, and put into a Pest House or Hospital, where he was convalescing for, and had the small Pox. that, after he had had the small Pox, his brother Stephen joined the army, which lay near New York, under Genl. Washington. This deponent says further, that he belonged, a part of the time, to the same Brigade, with his brother Stephen. and was with him, when not on duty, almost daily for many months at a time. that his brother was in the battle at Monmouth, which took place in 1778. and that he was in the battle at Long Island and Germantown.

