On this fourth day of April A.D. One Thousand eight hundred and fifty five personally appeared before me John Ward Master in Chancery, within and for the County and State aforesaid Chloe Butterfield aged seventy six years a resident of Springfield in the State of Vermont, who, being duly sworn according to law declares that she is the widow of William Butterfield, deceased, who was a Soldier in the American Army during the Revolutionary War - as appears by documents heretofore transmitted to the Department, on which under the Act of 3 d February 1853, her pension Certificate dated 9 th Feby. 1854, and numbered on the back thereof 3632 - for the sum of $44,92 per annum appears to have been issued. Said Certificate purporting to have been recorded on "the roll of Pensioners" under said Act, at "page 84, Vol. A."
She further states that she was married to the said William Butterfield in Rockingham, Vermont, on the day of A.D. by Hoyt Esq. a Justice of the Peace, and that her name before her said marriage was Chloe Randall, that her said Husband died at Rockingham aforesaid on the day of June A.D. and that she is now a widow. All of which will further appear from the documents accompanying her application for the pension herein before referred to.

