On this 31 st day of May A. D. 1861 personally appeared in open Court, before me Levi B. Turner, a judge in and for the County of Otsego, in the State of New York, Sarah Camp, of the town of Hartwick in Said County, aged seventy four years, being first duly sworn according to law, doth on her oath make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the provision made by the law of the United States, passed on the 3 rd day of February A. D. 1853, that she is the widow of Benjamin Camp, who was a Marine in the Navy of the United States in the War of the Revolution, and served on board the Ship Providence, about the year 1778, and for said service, he received a pension certificate from the Government of the United States at the rate of Ninety Eight dollars per Annum, under the act passed by Congress on the 7 th day of June 1832, and for proof of said service, she refers the Commissioner of Pensions, to the proof on file in the pension office at Washington, in the case & application of her husband, Benjamin Camp for a pension, and also, a certificate of pension issued to him by the department, of which the following is a true copy. War Department. Revolutionary Claim. I certify that in conformity with the law of the United States of the 7 th of June 1832. Benjamin Camp, of the State of New York, who was a Marine in the Navy of the Revolution, is entitled to receive Ninety Eight dollars per Annum, during his natural life, commencing on the 4 th of March 1831.
