Commonwealth of Massachusetts Suffolk County - } ss.
On this first day of September A.D. 1843, personally appeared before the Hon. John Gray Rogers one of the Justices of the Justices Court for the County of Suffolk, the same being a Court of Record, Mrs. Mary Carpenter a resident of Roxborough in the County of Norfolk, aged eighty three years, who, being first duly sworn, according to law, doth, on her oath, make the following declaration, in order to obtain the benefit of the provision made by the act of Congress, passed July 7 th 1838, entitled "An act granting half pay and pensions to certain widows;" and also of the joint resolution of 16 th August 1842, & the decisions founded upon said act and Resolution;
That she is the widow of Ezra Carpenter who was a Private in the Army of the Revolution and served according to the best of her recollections & beliefs as follows. He enlisted in the year 1775. from the town of Wenham (a part of said town now being called Wenham) for the eight months service under Capt. Oliver Pond. Went to Roxbury & from there to New York. At the close of the s aid service he again enlisted as she supposes under Capt. Pond for the year's service of 1776. and when his term of enlistment was out staid six weeks longer as many of the soldiers were leaving the Army. He was also at Rhode Island about one month. She remembers of his speaking of being in the Battles of Trenton, Princeton & at the taking of the Hessians. She further alleges that her said husband was a pensioner under the law of 7 th June 1832 at $74.67 per annum & for further particulars, would refer to his declaration, & the proof accompanying the same, now on file in the War Department,

Commonwealth of Massachusetts } ss. Norfolk County }
I Mary Carpenter of Hoxboro' in the County & Commonwealth above named aged Eightyfive years on oath testify and say, that I am the widow of Ezra Carpenter decd. who was a revolutionary soldier and a pensioner of the U.S.; that on the 1 st day of Sept. 1843. I applied for a pension under the law of 7 th July 1838. which application was duly forwarded to the Department & placed on file; that at a subsequent date additional evidence was forwarded in support of said application but that before her claim was finally adjusted the Act of 30 th April was passed which barred her right to receive a pension under said Act and her claim was subsequently allowed under the law of 3 rd March 1843. at the rate of $74.67 per annum and the object of this affidavit is to obtain such additional pension as she may be entitled to by the recent joint resolution of Congress.
