On the 22 d day of May 1837 personally appeared before me Robert McCalla a justice of the peace in and for said county Mary Chisham aged upwards of eighty two years - who being first duly sworn according to law - Doth on her oath make the following Declarations in order to obtain the benefit of the pension made by the act of Congress passed July the 4 th 1836. That she is the widow of George Chisham De d who was a private soldier in the army of the Revolutionary war who drew a pension from the United States of ninety six Dollars per annum under an act of Congress passed the 18 th March 1818 on account of services rendered during the Revolutionary war and for the truth of which she refers to papers on file in the pensions office and further That her husband George Chisham kept a daily account of all the transactions which took place during the time he was in the service - as well of the Battles in which he fought - as the country which he marched through all of which with the Bible in which their marriage was recorded and his discharge from the army - and all the household property which they owned was consumed by fire some years after he returned from the army - She further states that the Clerk's office of Orange County Va has been carefully searched and no record of their marriage can be found - But she well knows that she was legally married to the afforsaid George Chisham in the month of March 1770* She further states That she was married about three years before the birth of her first Child (George) which was bore on the 24 th day of August 1782 which will more fully appear from the present family record and she well recollects that her husband the afforsaid George Chisham left home to join the Army about six months before the birth of her son George and did not return until George was ten months old during which time she never heard from him until he came home with his discharge And she further Declares that her husband George Chisham Departed this life on the 13 th day of December 1830 and that She still remaining his widow ever since that period as well more
