On this 15 th day of April A.D. 1851, personally appeared, before me Thomas Maney , Judge of the 6 th Judicial Circuit, of the State of Tennessee , Allen Cotton , Admin- istrator of Priscilla Cotton dec d , and made oath in due form of law, that he is the Administrator of said Priscilla Cotton , who was the Widow of Thomas Cotton deceased , who was a Captain in the North Carolina Line , Army of the Revolution , and who died in Sumner County, Tennessee , many years ago, that he left a Widow, Priscilla Cotton aforesaid , who survived him until on or about the 1 st day of November A.D. 1843 , when she died, that since her death an application was made by her then surviving children, through her (then) Administrator Josiah Malton , for a Pension, which was allowed, and a Certificate of Pension was issued in her name, under Act of Congress of July 2 nd 1836 , at the rate of Two Hundred Dollars per annum, said Pension commencing on the 2 nd day of March A.D. 1831 , and terminating on the 1 st day of November A.D. 1843 , being the day of her death, (as declarant believes); That Declarant is informed, and believes, that the amount then allowed, was not as much as they were entitled to under the aforesaid Act of July 2 nd 1836 , and that he makes this Declaration for the purpose of obtaining an Increase of Pension under that Act, Declarant having always understood from his Mother, Priscilla Cotton aforesaid , that her husband, the aforesaid Thomas Cotton , served as a Captain for several years during the Revolutionary War , but Declarant can not of course state the precise duration of his service; That at the time, that the above mentioned application for Pension was made, the then surviving children of the said Thomas, and Priscilla Cotton , were all properly identified, and that, as Declarant supposes, the proofs of their identity is now on file, in the Pension Office, at Washington City, together with the proof of the marriage of the said Thomas, and Priscilla Cotton , all of which papers, Declarant believes, were forwarded by N. Fergusson, Esq.
