The subscriber, Administrator of the estate of Judith Day late of Enfield, N. H. dec ed , the widow of Timothy Day, who was a soldier in the Army of the Revolution and a N.H. pensioner of the United States at the rate of Eighty dollars per annum under the act of the 7 th June 1832, acknowledges that he has been informed by Messrs. Evans & Fand that the claim in right of said Judith Day to a pension under the act of the 7 th July 1838 and subsequent act has lately been admitted under their agency at the rate of $57.22 per annum, and the pension made payable to him as Administrator for the benefit of her chil- dren. That he has submitted the subject to them, or some of them, and in reference to which he has respectfully to say that they are not satisfied with the amount allowed, believing, as did their deceased Mother, that their father, the said Timothy Day, rendered the service for which he claimed and was allowed. And so believing myself, and that there is proof of the same on file, I have return- ed the certificate to said agents, praying a re-examina- tion of the papers that have heretofore been filed in the case and particularly the depositions of Edward Spalding

