State of New York - } Chautauque County ss. }
Sarah Fuller of the town of Pomfret in the County of Chautauque and State of New York, being duly sworn, doth depose and say, that on or about the 27. April, 1840, she made her Declaration before Elisha Ward, one of the Judges of the Court of Common Pleas of said County, in order to obtain the benefit of the Act of Congress, passed July 7. 1838, entitled, "An Act granting half pay and pensions to certain Widows" - That in that in that Declaration, (which she believes remains on file in the Pension Office,) the following facts are stated and set forth, viz. - That she was then a resident of the said town of Pomfret, and was 71 years of age on the 22 d August 1839 - That she was the widow of Daniel Fuller, who was a private in the service of the United States in the war of the Revolution, and that for his services, he received Eighty dollars per annum, under the Act of Congress, passed June 7. 1832 - That she drew about forty dollars of his Pension after his death - That she was married to the said Daniel Fuller on the 18 th day of October, 1785 - and that he (said Fuller) died August 27 th 1836 - and that she had remained a widow ever since his death - All which, amongst other things, will appear by reference to her said Declaration, And she says that all the above mentioned facts are true - and that the Record from the family Bible is true, as explained in the affidavit of her son Daniel, made & sworn to on the 13. May, 1845, before Philo Orton, Justice of the Peace - And she says that she has not intermarried, but continues the widow of the said Daniel Fuller - and is a resident of the said town of Pomfret - And she now asks for a Pension under the Act of July 7. 1838, of Eighty dollars per annum, for five years, commencing on the 4 th March, 1836, and terminating on the 4 th March, 1841; and also that she may be allowed a Pension, under subsequent Acts, of Eighty dollars per annum, for five years, commencing on the 4 th March, 1843, and terminating on the 4 th March, 1848 -
Sworn to and subscribed before me, } a Justice of the Peace of Chautauque County, N.Y. June 14 th 1845 }
Philo Orton Justice of Peace Sarah Fuller
Witness - Jacob Houghton.
I certify that I am personally acquainted with the above named Sarah Fuller, and that I believe her statements true - And I note that the words "in that" are one word - and that the words "he" - "Bible", interlined, and part of the word "Daniel" written on margin, in the affidavit - & "of his" interlined Dated June 14 th 1845. Philo Orton Justice of Peace

