On this 27 th day of September A.D. 1855, before the County Court of Barren County held within and for the County and State aforesaid, personally appeared Elizabeth Hooin aged 62 years, a resident of the County of Barren and State of Kentucky; who being duly sworn according to law, doth, on her oath, make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the provision made by the act of Congress, passed on the 3 rd February, 1853; granting pensions to widows of persons who served during the Revolutionary War; that she is the widow of John Hooin deceased, who was a Revolutionary pensioner under the act of Congress passed June 7 th 1832, at the rate of thirty dollars and eighty eight cents per annum - which was paid him at the Pensions agency in the State of Kentucky; for proof of her said husband's services she refers to his papers now on file in the Pension office - She further states that she was married to the said John Hooin in Barren County State of Kentucky on the 26 th day of May A.D. 1826; by one Jacob Socke a Baptist M.; and that her name before her said marriage was Elizabeth Dursall; that her husband died at Barren County Kentucky on the day of August A.D. 1836. That she was not married to him prior to the second of January eight hundred, but at the time aforesaid, and she further states that there is a public record of her marriage, and a private record of her husband's death both of which are now on file at the Pension office in Washington D.C. and which she filed with the Declaration by her for Bounty Land under the act of September 28-1850 - And she further states that she has never married since her said husband's death, but is still his widow -

State of Virginia County of Amherst }
Before me Zack D'Ansley — a Justice of the Peace within and for the county and State aforesaid, on this eighth day of December 1855, personally appeared James Grant — and Patrick H Grant of Amherst county, Virginia, of lawful age, to me well known to be persons of veracity, and whose testimony is entitled to full faith and credit, who having been duly sworn according to law, depose as follows:
That they are neighbors, and are well acquainted with Susanna Grant, widow of Robert Grant, deceased, and with her husband in his life time; that they lived together as man and wife from the first of their recollection until his death, and raised a large family of children, who are recognized as such, and that they never heard it questioned or denied by anyone that they were legally married; that the said Robert Grant, her husband, was in receipt of a pension as a Revolutionary soldier, for twenty dollars per annum, and that they have seen the certificate of pension; that the said Robert Grant died in Amherst county, Virginia on the 20th day of July, 1838, and that Susanna Grant is his widow, not having been married since his death. That they are in no wise interested in this claim for a pension for Susanna

