On this 27th day of January A.D. 1860 personally appeared before me William M. Wiley a Justice of the Peace within and for the County and State aforesaid Mrs. Hannah Hervey a resident of Liconduoga in the County of Essex and State of New York aged ninety years who being duly sworn according to law doth on her oath make the following decla- ration in order to obtain the benefit of the provision made by the act of Congress passed on the 3rd of February 1853 granting pensions to widows of persons who served during the Revolutionary War that she is the widow of Paul Hervey deceased who was a Private in the War of the Revolution. That by reason of age and the loss of memory she cannot tell when her said husb- and entered the service, how long he continued in, or the time he remained in said war, nor the time he was discharged therefrom. That her said husband was a pensioner of the United States under act of 7th June 1832 at the rate of thirty dollars per annum which was paid to him at the Agency in the State of New York. She further states that she was married to the said Paul Hervey in Harwich in Massachusetts on the 19th day of March 1798 by the Rev. Mr. Simpkins a Minister of the Gospel and that her name before her said marriage was * that her husband died at Liconduoga on the 4th day of May 1859 Hannah Nickerson. That she was not married to him previous to but at the time above stated and she further states that there is no public Record of her Marriage and that there is a private Record of her marriage in her family bible (which is herewith sent) That she cannot file herewith his Certificate of discharge for the reason that she does not know or remember as he ever had one and further she declares that she is now a widow

