On this 3 rd day of October in the year 1848. Veneratingly appeared before me the undersigned a Justice of the Peace for the County and state aforesaid, Mrs. Rebecca Hemwood, Widow of Robert Hemwood who was a Private of Armands Leguis. who being duly sworn according to law, declares and swears, on her oath that under the different acts of Congress, that is the act of 7 June 1838 and of the 3 rd March 1843, and the 17 June 1844 she drew a pension of 80 ts per year, and that she now claims 20 ts per annum, in addition to what she has already received by virtue of the aforesaid acts of Congress, as she has understood that Mrs Nancy Rock, widow of John Rock, and Mrs Mary Curtis, Widow of Roland Curtis and Mrs Margaret Carr, formerly Widow of William Brown of Knox County Tennessee all of whom belonged to Armands Leguis or Cox during the Revolutionary War, have each drawn a pension of One Hundred Dollars per annum as she is informed, and that she thinks that she is entitled to the same amount of pension that they are, as her said Husband belonged to the Cor, and she further declares that she is still a Widow.
Sworn to and subscribed this day and year above written.
Rebecca her Hemwood mark
