State of Maine } ss County of Somerset }
Be It Known That on this Seventh day of July A.D. eighteen hundred and fifty three Daniel White Judge of Probate in and for said County and State aforesaid personally appeared Anna Hinds a resident of Madison in said County and Made oath according to Law. That she is the widow of Benjamin Hinds of Madison, who was a Soldier in the war of the Rebellion and a pensioner of the United States at the time of his death at the rate of $96 per annum that she was married to the said Benjamin Hinds on the twenty first of December 1817 in the town of Bridgewater and then the State of Massachusetts that his said husband died on the 29 th day of March eighteen hundred and thirty six. She makes this declaration for the purpose of obtaining such amount of pension as may be due under any of the laws regulating or decisions that have been or may hereafter be adopted. Anna Hinds
The said declaration further states that she hereby constitutes and appoints Peter Boston her true and lawful attorney and Agent to prosecute said claim with power of substitution to examine all papers and documents in relation to it, and to do all things necessary and lawful in premises except drawing the money which will be hereafter paid to her for Anna Hinds

State of New York } ss County of Rensselaer }
On this 11 th day of November 1857. Personally appeared before the Subordinate one of the Justices of the Peace Court in the City of Troy County aforesaid, the said being a Court of Record, duly authorized by law to administer Oaths Hannah Burns aged 70 years, a resident of Larringtonburgh County and State aforesaid. Who being duly sworn according to law doth on her Oath make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the Provisions made by the Act of Congress passed on the 3 rd of February 1853. Entitled Pensions to Widows of Persons who served during the Revolutionary War that Husbands the Widow of James Hodge who was a Soldier in said War, and who drew a Pension of $96. per annum under the Act of 1818 - was placed on the Pension Roll 23 rd September 1818. And died the 3 rd day of July 1821 She further declares that she was married to said Hodge about two years before his death by a Justice of the Peace named Cheak, who is dead. That after the death of Hodge she married John Burns in Larringtonburgh County aforesaid by Richard L. McDonald a Justice of the Peace cannot tell the time as I can not tell and know of no Record. That the said Burns died on the 3 rd day of November 1857. That she was not married to said Hodge previous to the 2 nd of January Eighteen Hundred and that she is now a Widow. Hannah Burns Subscribed and sworn on the day and year above written before me. P. McDonald Justice
I certify that it has been shown before me by the testimony of Philip G. Miller and Jacob Morris Residents of Rensselaer County aforesaid. known to me as credible Person. That James Hodge and Hannah Burns were always reported as Husband & Wife lived together as such until his death. That he died the 3 rd day of July 1821. and that said Hannah was married to John Burns - who died the 3 rd day of November 1857 And I further certify. That from bodily infirmity said Hannah was not able to attend Court and could not write her name P. McDonald Justice

State of New York } ss. Rensselaer County }
On this fifteenth day of July One Thousand eight hundred and fifty four. Personally appeared before the Justices of the Peace, Court in the City of Troy County of Rensselaer State aforesaid now sitting, the said being a Court of Record, duly authorized by Law to administer Oaths, Christopher Hedges a resident of Rensselaer County aforesaid aged 85 years, who being duly sworn according to law, testifies and makes the following Declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the Acts of Congress, making provision of certain Widows of Soldiers of the Revolutionary War. Dated July 7 th 1838. March 3 d 1843 - Jan 17 th 1844 And July 2 d 1848 That I the widow of Daniel Hedges who was a Pensioner of the Revolution under the Act of 1832 of Twenty Two dollars and thirty two cents per Annum That I was married to said Hedges in the then County of Albany now Rensselaer County by a Justice of the Peace, who I understand is dead and left no record
That our husband died on the 9 th day of June 1832, and the name of the Public and the Family Record of the

State of Massachusetts County of Bristol
} ss.
On thirteenth  twenty fifth day of June AD, 1853, before me, the undersigned, a judge of the Bristol County Probate Court of the State of Mas- sachusetts, Mrs. Statia Holden, a resident of Pawtucket in the County of Bristol and State aforesaid, aged 78 years, per- sonally appeared, who first being duly sworn according to law, doth, on her oath, make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefits of the provisions made by the act of Congress, passed on the 3 rd of February 1853, granting pensions to widows of persons who served during the Revolutionary war; that she is the widow of Daniel Holden, who was a Bombardier in Cramers Artillery in the Mass. line, in the Revolutionary war, and who was inscribed on the Maine Pension Roll, under the act of Congress passed May 15 th 1828, at the rate of one hundred and eight dollars per annum, - paid at the Portland Maine Agency - And for proof of his said service, she refers to her said husbands dec- laration under said act, and other evidence.

