State of Maine } ss. County of Oxford }
On this fourteenth day of June 1848, personally appeared before me, Job Prince a justice of the Peace in and for the County aforesaid, Mercy Holmes a resident of Hartford in the County of Oxford aged seventy nine years, who being first duly sworn, according to law, doth on her oath, make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefits of the provision made by the Act of Congress, passed on the 2 d February 1848, granting pensions to widows of persons who served during the Revolutionary war. That she is the widow of Jonathan Holmes who was a Sergeant in the Army of the Revolution, and for proof of the service of her late husband, as well as to the Regiment or Corps to which he belonged &c. She would refer the Department to her declaration and the evidence accompanying the same upon which she was granted a Pension of one hundred twenty dollars per Annum, under the Act of July 7 th 1838, and 3 d March 1843 & June 17 th 1844, the last Certificate of which was numbered 2265 being an increase from $88.00 per Annum to $120.00 - She further declares that she is still a widow. Witness: Isaac Holmes Mercy Holmes mark
Sworn to and subscribed on the day and year first above written, by said declarant (who is personally known to me) Before me John Prince justice of the peace

State of New York County of Columbia } ss. On this 13 th day of April A.D. one thousand eight hundred and fifty five personally appeared before me Wheeler H. Clarke a Commissioner of Ducts in the City of Hudson County of Columbia and State aforesaid Hannah Holsapple a widow of Ghent, County of Columbia and State of New York, who being first duly sworn according to law declares that she is the identical Hannah Holsapple who is the widow of John Holsapple who was a Soldier of the Revolution. She is now 86 years old;
That she the declarant is a pensioner of the United States under act of 2 nd February 1848 at the rate of $28.14 per annum on account of her said husband's revolutionary services. That she still remains the widow of said John Holsapple not having intermarried since his death. That her pension certificate, is dated the 2 nd day of December 1848 and is numbered 2260, and recorded in Volume 4, page 83.
She further says that she refers to the proof on file in her application for her said pension to prove the services of her said husband in the war of the Revolution, the time of her marriage and the time of her husband's death. She makes this declaration for the

State of Georgia } County of DeKalb. }
On this seventh day of January in the year of our Lord Eighteen
hundred & thirty nine, personally appeared before the Court of Ordinary in & for the State & County of and Elizabeth Hooper a resident of the said County & State aged Eighty six years who being first duly sworn according to law doth on her oath make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the provisions made by the act of Congress passed July the 4th 1836. That she is the widow of James Hooper who was a private in the war of the Revolution, and who performed such service in said war as are des- cribed in his declaration now of file in the war Department at the City of Washington for which the said James Hooper had at the time of his death a pension certificate numbered 12,789 and dated on the 24th day of May 1833 and signed by Lewis Cass Secretary of war, and counter- signed by J. L. Edwards Commissioner of pensions, by which it appears that the said James Hooper was entitled to receive the sum of fifty three dollars & thirty three cents per annum during his natural life, she further declares that she was married to the said James Hooper in the year on the sixth day of January in the year 1781, that her husband the aforesaid James Hooper died on the 28th day of April in the year 1836 and that she has remained a widow ever since that period, as will more fully appear by reference to

State of Virginia } City of Richmond }
On this 15 th day of May in the year 1854 personally appeared before the Circuit Court for the City of Richmond, and the State of Virginia. Mary Ann Hooper, a resident of the City of Richmond, and State afore said, aged about 60 years, who first being duly sworn according to law, doth, on her oath, make the following declaration in order to obtain the benefits of the provision made by the act of Congress, passed 3 rd February 1853; granting pensions to widows of persons who served during the Revolutionary War: That she is the widow of James Hooper who was a Revolutionary Pensioner at the rate of $80 per annum, under the act of Congress ap- pro-ved on the 15 th day of May 1828: from Henrico County Virginia, and who is now residing in the refer- red in the paper, which accompanies this decla- -ration Certified to be a true copy by George H. Mumford, Sect y of the Comm ee . Cramp date after 29 th 1854
She further declares that she was married to the said James Hooper, in the County of Henrico on the 20 th day of October Eighteen hundred and Twenty Nine, and that her name before marriage was Mary Ann Childers. That her said hus- band died on the 10 th day of January Eighteen hundred and thirty three; that she was not marri- ed to him prior to the second day of January Eighteen hundred, but at the time above mention- ed, she further declares that she is now a widow.

State of New Jersey } Middlesex County }
Supplemental declaration of Dorothea Nortwick
On this twentieth day of June one thousand eight hundred and forty four person-ally appeared before the subscriber one of the Judges of the Inferior Court of Common Pleas in and for said County Dorothea Nortwick a resident of North Brunswick in the County and State aforesaid in the Seventy eighth year of her age a person of veracity who being first duly sworn according to law doth on her oath make the following supplemental declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the provision made by the act of Congress Passed July 7th 1838 Entitled "an act granting half pay and Pensions to certain widows," That she is the widow of Barret Nortwick who was a fifer in Capt Helms company of the second Jersey Regiment in the War of the Revolution being a war man this declarant has received Eighty dollars per annum under said act the pay of a Private and as her said husband was a musician she asks such an increase as is justly due her on account of the grade her said husband filled in said Revolution For such other facts as related to their said marriage as she refers to her declaration on file at the Pension Department and that she still remains a widow
Sworn and subscribed on the day and year last above written
Before Peter L. Kenyon } Judge }
Dorothea X Nortwick mark

