State of Connecticut } ss. County of Tolland }
On this 6 th day of September in the year One thousand Eight hundred and fifty-five. Before the subscriber a Justice of the Peace within & for the County aforesaid duly authorized by law to administer Oaths. Personally came Freelove Hull aged 83 years a resident of the town of Hebron in the County & State aforesaid who being duly sworn according to law Declares and says that she is the identical Freelove Hull who is the widow of Joseph Hull who was a Lieutenant and Captain in the Revolutionary War in the Service of the United States, That she is a Revolutionary Pension of the United States at the rate of three hundred and thirty two dollars & thirty five cents per annum, on account of the services of said Joseph Hull. That she was first pensioned under the act of February 3 d 1853 at the rate of three hundred and twenty dollars per annum. That her pension was raised from said sum of 320. dollars per ann. to said sum of 332 \frac{35}{100} dollars per annum on account of her husband the said Joseph Hull being a Lieutenant of Artillery, instead of a Lieutenant of Infantry. That her "Pension" is not a full Pension of a Lieutenant of Artillery, and she therefore asks that she may be placed on the Pension Roll at the rate of Four Hundred and Forty dollars per annum from the 3 d of February 1853. That she understands & believes that her husband the said Joseph Hull performed other service than that counted to her in making said Pension Certificate, "especially in the year 1781 under "Colonel Jon o Fitch of Connecticut as Captain or (Lieutenant) of Whales Boats, in the Independent Volunteers." That she is now a widow, has not intermarried but is now the widow of said Joseph Hull. That she continues
